<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Has ~ one relation, three slots, and it fuses nothing

`has` carries one relation: an ''ENTITY'', a ''NAME'' under that entity, and ''what that
name holds''. The held thing answers for itself; the edge keeps silent.

```
<<~ has rank pulse "levels/0-4 reads/a-mark-a-morpheme-a-word">>
```

Where prose fuses map to territory (`X ${copula} Y`, the dead water), `has` re-opens
the hand: a name //holds// what it holds, keeps the two apart, leaves the holding
droppable, and rests the name's essence nowhere. A holding sets down without wounding
what held it; a name that drops one keeps its address and keeps its name.

''The entity rides FIRST, so one definition answers for every family.'' The boot seats
eight of them on the relation directly — `mu` · `carry` · `persona` · `frame` ·
`season` · `config` · `integrity` · `shrine` — and the corpus authors rows under
seventy-five entities besides. Each names a region; each writes the same three slots.
The boot seed states the relation once, and the shelf answers for it everywhere else.

''Two regions wear the same word, and the field parts them.'' `has` authors a row in a
carrier's ''body'' — an entity a reader meets in prose. `has-stack` governs a carrier's
''own'' stack in the meta `tags` field — what THIS carrier wears. One law reads what a
body says; the other reads what a carrier composes from.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! Three clauses hold the law

# ''Has carries what a name holds, and refuses essence.'' `<<~ has E N "…">>` writes
   what the name `N` under the entity `E` holds; the pair holds it, and never turns
   into it. A line that reads `E N holds Y` where prose fused `E N ${copula} Y` clears
   the ward, and the held thing answers for itself.

# ''Header-and-rows tables fall to has by one rule.'' The header vows the families (the
   column grain); each body row authors one call, writing one `family/cell` per column it
   fills. A row skips the columns it leaves empty — open-world omission, never a null.
   The boot's carrier-tables fall to this rule untouched by hand.

# ''Order, crossing and grade each ride their own verb.'' `has` carries what a name
   holds and states no order over it. A relation that //sequences//, //crosses// or
   //grades// slips the dead water past a `has` row; `#/when-not-has` routes each to
   its verb.

''A row's steering data rides the `~` red aside'' (`element/Fire ~ rating/GR2` —
confidence · provenance · resolved-status): red annotates the row, never black-asserts
it, and the aside stays out of the carrier's canonical bytes.

''A row states a holding; it opens no query face.'' The predicate runs over the `tags`
field instead, through the filter `has-stack` names — `[stack[]]` / `[stack:has[<name>]]`.

<<~ pranala #shelf from="?" -> to="packages/lararium-tw5/tiddlers/sigil-has.tid" family="code" role="has">>

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-three-slots>>

!! The three slots, and the three laws that decide what a slot receives

| ''slot'' | ''carries'' | ''shape'' |
|1 · ENTITY |the family — the region the row belongs to |one word |
|2 · NAME |the row — the member within that family |one word |
|3 · HOLDS |what that name holds, whole |ONE quoted string |

''One relation names every family, and the family rides as its first word.''

''AND THE SPACE AFTER THE SHARKTOOTH BINDS.'' `lar-sigil` claims every `<<~` — the spaced
form through its compound branch, the tight form through the generic fallback — but only
the compound branch reaches a DEFINITION. Measured at the render:

|! call |! page |
|`<<~ has Verb run "args/x">>` |`Verb run holds [args/x]` |
|`<<~has Verb run "args/x">>` |`&lt;&lt;~has Verb run "args/x"&gt;&gt;` |

A claim by the rule and a call to the definition name different acts, and the tight form
performs the first alone. Three hundred and sixty-five rows crossed to `has` wearing the
tight spelling, each one reading live on the page and reaching nothing.

''THE THIRD SLOT RIDES QUOTED.'' It carries slashes, colons, arrows and prose. A call
binds a named parameter with `=`, and TiddlyWiki reads any unquoted `word:` as exactly
that — so a bare third slot hands its payload to a parameter nobody named and leaves the
positional ''EMPTY''. One pair of quotes makes the whole payload ONE value.

''AND IT RENDERS AS TEXT.'' What a row holds carries square brackets, arrows and
backticks. A filtered transclusion would render each as a LINK to a tiddler nobody
wrote, and wikitext would read `[Fiduciary: POA]` as a wikilink. A value names no
address; the shelf renders the slot through `<$text>` so the bytes a reader typed reach
the page unchanged.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/table-lift>>

!! A table falls to has ~ a worked lift

The Mu table, as a table — the header vows, the cells honor by position:

| name | ascii | element | glyph | pull |
|---|---|---|---|---|
|Wand |* |Fire |🜂 |ignition |
|Arcana |0 |Orichalcum |🜍 |reset |

The same fact, as rows — ''one relation, and the family rides as its first word'':

<<~ has Mu Wand "ascii/* glyph/🜂 element/Fire pull/ignition">>
<<~ has Mu Arcana "ascii/0 glyph/🜍 element/Orichalcum pull/reset">>

The Wand-row wears all five; the Arcana-row wears the ground member's own, written where
a reader can see them. Neither row carries a null, and neither leans on a constructor.

''A DEFAULT LIVES IN A DEFINITION WHILE A CARRIER HOLDS NO DEFINITIONS.'' A row leaning
on a default reads empty to everyone who never opens the constructor. Every row writes
what it holds where a reader can see it.

A holding too wide for one line takes a second call under the same entity and name,
never a truncation: the slot states no order, so what one call writes two calls write.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/when-not-has>>

!! When not to reach for has ~ the other copula-free grammars

`has` cures one fused relation: ''identity'' (`X ${copula} Y`, map welded to territory).
The dead water welds //other// relations too — sequence, crossing, grade — and `has`
flattens each. A row that //orders//, //moves// or //ranks// wants its own verb. Reach
past `has` only once a row fails the ''holds-test''.

!!! The diagnostic ~ ask one question

Ask of the row's relation: ''what does it DO?'' The answer routes the verb.

| ''when the relation…'' | ''rides…'' | ''authors as…'' | ''and cures the dead water of…'' |
|''holds · wears'' //(default)// |a holding under a name |`<<~ has entity name "family/member …">>` |identity |
|''follows · flows'' |a phase chain |`<<~ loops "a -> b -> c">>` |sequence-fused-as-identity |
|''grades · ranks'' |a gradation |`<<~ scale family "a ~ one -> b ~ two">>` |level-welded-to-meaning |
|''moves across'' |a transition |a trigger · a guard · an effect, each named |before-welded-to-after |

`has` carries most of the boot; the others carry a minority — the ordered runs, the
graded registers, the phase changes. Default to `has`; reach past it only once the
holds-test fails.

!!! A run that FOLLOWS ~ the phase chain (sequence rides content)

A run whose order carries meaning — the Ladders, OODA-HA, the boot-chain — moves as
sequence, never as composition. The arrow names the pull: a chain //leans on verbs, reads
left-to-right// and never folds inside-out. TW5 carries this in the `list` field (which
runs apart from `tags`), walked first-to-last.

```
<<~ loops "✶ observe -> ⏿ orient -> ◇ decide -> ▶ act -> ↺ aftermath">>
```

The arrow //moves//; nothing welds to anything. The tail MAY re-enter the head — OODA-HA
keeps its ↺ Aftermath glyph as a phase. Each ladder earns its own meme; `loci` carries
the address.

!!! A run that GRADES ~ the gradation verb (asymmetric relation)

A scale that ranks — the Syad registers, the stage bands — moves as gradation. The
ordinal anchor: a rank //exceeds// another by an unknown span, and no level //welds// to
its meaning. The Evidence Question — //how do I know?// — hands over the verb.

```
<<~ scale syad "literal@0 ~ unsupported -> figurative@20 ~ fully-confirmed">>
<<~ scale stage "green-room@1..4 -> wings@5..12 -> downstage@13..20">>
<<~ scale rating "noise -> data -> meme -> ano -> kapu">>
```

Anchors read `member[@coord][~ descriptor]` — `@n` a point, `@lo..hi` a band; the order
ascends, never sorted. A descriptor //describes// what a level looks like; it rides the
droppable red aside and never welds the number to the meaning.

!!! A row that MOVES ACROSS ~ the transition (trigger · guard · effect)

A relation that carries a thing //across// conditions — a phase change, a degraded-state
recovery — moves as transition. The state-machine grammar runs verb-forward by nature: a
transition names ''what fires it, what gates it, what it does'', and never pronounces the
after welded to the before. The trigger does the work the dead water would have smuggled.

<<~/ahu>>

